{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And put it in the ears of, etc.</b> One needs to know why this statement was directed to Joshua alone. And perhaps it is because, since Joshua perceived the intensity of Amalek's fury and strength, and he did not have the power to bring about their complete annihilation but only weakened them, and in the heart of the righteous one there was the thought — how did HaShem not do what is fitting to do to this wicked one? — therefore HaShem came to relieve his distress and commanded Moses to say into his ear — meaning, that this matter is to respond to what was in Joshua's heart — and he told him not to worry about the matter: ״מָחֹה אֶמְחֶה״ etc., and the word of HaShem is as though it has already come to pass, and this shall be his consolation." "And the reason for the doubling of ״מָחֹה״ etc. — perhaps it alludes to us the times in which He will blot them out: (a) in the days of Saul, corresponding to which He said ״מָחֹה״; (b) in the days of Mordecai, corresponding to which He said ״אֶמְחֶה״; and (c) once for the future, corresponding to which He said ״מִתַּחַת הַשָּׁמָיִם״." "<b>Thus concludes the perasha of Beshalach</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:19.837528488Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}